    "result_text": "All tests pass. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in `OwnerTelephones.java`, the single normalization/validation rule for owner telephones.\n\n**The change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephones.java`):\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` map (country calling code \u2192 required national digits): `+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10. It's a `LinkedHashMap` ordered longest-code-first so the most specific calling code matches (e.g. `61` before `1`).\n- Added a small private helper `requireCountryNationalLength(digits)` that, for a recognised country code prefix, computes the national part length (total digits minus the code length) and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) when it isn't exactly what that country requires. Unrecognised codes keep the existing generic 8\u201315 digit check.\n\nI kept the logic inside the existing `OwnerTelephones` unit (its stated responsibility is exactly \"the single normalization rule for owner telephones\") rather than scattering it, and factored the new rule into its own focused method so `toE164` stays readable. Nothing else needed changing \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` already routes through `toE164`, and the exception already maps to a 400.\n\n**Verification:** the new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 3 national digits \u2192 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The valid-owner generator's `04XXXXXXXX` numbers still pass: dropping the leading `0` gives 9 national digits under `+61`, exactly as required.",
